#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the Gorakṣaśataka, 2026-10-17
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:NathMisslingenSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every caution, condition of failure and named failure in the Gorakṣaśataka"@en, "Jede Warnung, Misslingensbedingung und benannte Misslingensform im Gorakṣaśataka"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Gorakṣaśataka, vv. 1-101, read complete in G. W. Briggs's romanized text and English translation (Gorakhnāth and the Kānphaṭa Yogīs, ch. 14); every passage found checked in Mahīdhara's Hindi in the Bombay Gorakṣapaddhati, first hundred, and at the parallels in the 2025 English translation of the Vivekamārtaṇḍa."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any statement that a practice taught in the work can harm the practitioner or fail to bring about what it is practised for, any sign by which a failure would be recognised, and any account of why one would happen."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Sanskrit: katham sidhyanti, bandhanāya, mūḍhānām, gopanīyā, na deyā, tadaiva, kṣamaḥ, pathyam apathyam, nātra kāryā vicāraṇā; in Briggs's English: how can, bondage, fools, secret, not to be revealed, capable, no doubt; in the Hindi: बंधन, मूर्ख, गुप्त, सामर्थ्यहीन, अन्यथा नहीं, संदेह न करना."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NathSiddhiPresupposesDehajnana, pmc:NathPranaNirodhaPresupposesNadisuddhi, pmc:NathSiddhatvaPresupposesBrahmacarya, pmc:NathKundaliniBandhana, pmc:NathMahamudraGopaniyata ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-17" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-17" ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure, both conditions of possibility and both decided by the practitioner: perfection does not come to one who does not know the centres of his own body, and the breath cannot be held until the channels are clean. One caution against the central practice, that kuṇḍalinī binds the foolish, with no mark of who is foolish. One restriction on passing on. No sign of failure, no case of failure, and no explanation of a failure: the work names nothing that would show a practice had not worked, and the Hindi commentator adds at the year's term that one is not to doubt."@en .

pmp:NathKalaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Gorakṣaśataka names a term within which an effect is to come"@en, "Ob das Gorakṣaśataka eine Frist nennt, binnen derer eine Wirkung kommen soll"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term in days, months or years for any effect the work promises, a count of practitioners, or a number that could be checked."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Sanskrit: abdād ūrdhvam, māsatrayād ūrdhvataḥ, ṣaṇmāsa, sata ṣaṭśatāni, ekaviṃśati, ṣaṭtriṃśad aṅgula; in the English: after a year, after three months, six months, twenty-one thousand six hundred, thirty-six fingers; in the Hindi: एक वर्ष, तीन महीने, छः महीने."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NathSaktiPrabodhanaSiddhatva, pmc:NathNadisodhanaNadisuddhi, pmc:NathKhecariPresupposesChedana ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-17" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-17" ;
    skos:note "Two terms in the work itself, a year for perfection under the conditions of chastity and measure, and three months for the channels; one in the Hindi commentary's insertion, six months for preparing the tongue. The counts of the breath, twenty-one thousand six hundred a day and thirty-six fingers' breadth, describe the body and promise nothing. No term is set for any of the claims about death, poison, sleep, youth or release."@en .

pmp:NathLaksanaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every sign the Gorakṣaśataka announces for its practices"@en, "Jedes Zeichen, das das Gorakṣaśataka für seine Übungen ankündigt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses, and the second hundred of the Bombay Gorakṣapaddhati at the section on prāṇāyāma."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any perception, sensation or bodily change the work tells the practitioner to expect as a mark that the practice is working."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Sanskrit: jyotis, maṇivat, nādābhivyakti, analasya pradīpanam, yatheṣṭaṃ dhāraṇam, svedaḥ, kampaḥ, uttiṣṭhati, sthānam āpnoti; in the English: light, jewel, internal sound, digestive fire, perspiration, trembling, floats; in the Hindi: पसीना, कंप, उठता, नाद."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:NathNadisuddhiLaksana, pmc:NathPranayamaTrividhaLaksana ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-17" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-17" ;
    skos:note "Four signs of purified channels in the work, and three graded signs of breath restraint in the Gorakṣapaddhati's second hundred and in a verse its commentary quotes, sweat, trembling and rising. The light seen in samādhi is the content of a practice and is entered as one, not as a sign. Seven signs against no sign of failure."@en .
